  "description": "O3' selects a block, but NOT the bound state's block. The objective t_lambda = w_lambda * chi^lambda(W_atom) factors into an atom-INDEPENDENT weight and an atom-dependent character; measured over all six committed rows, argmin(t) == argmin(w) every time, so the character never moves the selection. Two atoms at n=3 and two at n=9 give the SAME block with DIFFERENT prices. Mechanism UNEXPLAINED: the first spread-dominance account is refuted here -- chi-spread exceeds w-spread at n=9 and the argmin is unmoved anyway. THE MECHANISM THAT DOES hold is an ANTI-CORRELATION between w and chi: concordant partition pairs are 9/49 = 18% against 24.5 expected, so a large character lands preferentially on a small weight and the product is FLATTENED relative to w on every row (never amplified) -- chi is a partial cancellation of the ordering it would have to reverse, not an independent perturbation. Exact distribution-free tail, one row per distinct n and the least extreme within each: 343/518400 ~ 6.6e-4. Cancellation is INCOMPLETE (t-spread 1.35x-10.2x), so w's argmin survives. WHY the anti-correlation holds REDUCES to the content sum: w is a strictly increasing function of c(lambda) on 49/49 pairs, so conc(w,|chi|) == conc(c(lambda),|chi|) IDENTICALLY row by row, and the effect decomposes as c up => w up (49/49 exact, 0 ties), c up => dim down (6/48), dim up => |chi| up (43/48 = 89.6%). For two-row partitions [n] has max content and dim 1 while the balanced partition has min content and max dim. CORRECTED here: those last two denominators read 49 and n=6 was called 'uncorrelated at 3/6' -- a tie ([5,1] and [3,3] both have f^lambda = 5 at n=6, the only tie in the sample) was being counted as discordant; n=6 is 3/5 = 60%, weakly POSITIVE. The two exact claims are untouched because c(lambda), w and |chi| are tie-free, now asserted. SETTLED NEGATIVELY: the dim->|chi| link is STATISTICAL, not structural -- rows 3 and 4 are two atoms at n=9 with the identical dimension multiset {1,8,27,42,48} and disagree (10/10 vs 9/10), which no partition-only law could do. The 5 discordant pairs sit on three partitions: [4,2] at n=6, [7,2] at n=9, [7,5] at n=12. THE Q-DIMENSION IS NOT THE MISSING notion -- measured at the proton q0, dim_q scores 43/49 (asym) and 44/49 (sym) against f^lambda's 43/48, i.e. noise, so that repair is refuted. MOST IMPORTANT, AND IT WEAKENS THIS PROBE'S OWN HEADLINE: the selection MARGIN t(runner-up)/t(argmin) is only 1.20x-2.11x, and in 5 of 6 rows it is SMALLER than that row's own character spread (at n=12, 2.05x against 12.5x). The large weight gap (2.0x-17.3x) is nearly cancelled by the character anti-correlation (0.77x-0.12x), leaving a thin win. So argmin(t)==argmin(w) in 6/6 is a MEASUREMENT, not a structural law with room to spare, and must not be quoted as one; an earlier version of this probe called the selection 'stable rather than noise', which was too strong and is corrected. TWO MORE CANDIDATE explanations for the imperfect dim link are REFUTED here: |chi|/dim is NOT monotone in dim (4/6 rows) and spans up to 33x within one row, so 'character = dimension x slowly varying factor' fails and no better notion of dimension repairs it. CAUTION: the CLASSICAL bound |chi| <= dim is VIOLATED in 6/6 rows (max |chi|/dim = 1.12 to 2.42, always at the one-row partition [n] where dim=1) -- so this field is not a classical S_n character. NOT a new corpus finding, and my first wording over-claimed it as one: magnus_fs_transpose_prune_probe already measures a DIFFERENT normalised quantity reaching 29.0 and already scopes Feray-Sniady to q=1. CONSEQUENCE: PR #6752's reading of the selection as 'which Wedderburn blocks the bound state saturates' is WRONG and is corrected; the pricing result (y_0* nonzero and atom-dependent, where the full LP has y_0* = 0 identically) STANDS.",
  "auditOnly": "docs/proposals/2026-09-05-operator-selection-objective-replacement.md",
